To handle complex, multi-faceted prompts—such as executing concurrent coding, security scanning, and documentation generation—your Agentic Orchestration Swarm cannot rely on rigid, linear execution. Instead, it must treat the compiled prompt as a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG).

Here is how a Tier-1 architecture orchestrates a multi-step dependency graph for concurrent coding, security, and documentation tasks.

1. The Core Paradigm: Dynamic DAG Generation
When the Meta-Prompt Optimizer compiles the user's rough prompt, it outputs not just a text prompt, but a Task Dependency Matrix.

For a request like "Build a user auth endpoint, scan it for vulnerabilities, and write the Swagger docs", the orchestrator generates a graph with three primary nodes:

Node A: The Coder Agent (Generates the implementation code)

Node B: The Security Auditor Agent (Performs static analysis and vulnerability checks)

Node C: The Documentation Writer Agent (Generates API specs, Readmes, and code comments)

2. Execution Flow: The Fan-Out / Fan-In Pattern
To maximize speed and efficiency, the swarm executes tasks concurrently wherever dependencies allow, using a Fan-Out / Fan-In pattern:

                  +---------------------------+
                  |  1. Intent & Task Parser  |
                  +-------------+-------------+
                                |
                                v
                  +---------------------------+
                  |      2. Coder Agent       |  <-- (Sequential Start)
                  +-------------+-------------+
                                |
                        (Code Artifact Ready)
                                |
            +-------------------+-------------------+
            | (Fan-Out)                             | (Fan-Out)
            v                                       v
+-----------------------+               +-----------------------+
| 3. Security Auditor   |               | 4. Doc Writer Agent   |
|    (SAST / Check)     |               |  (API Specs / Readme) |
+-----------+-----------+               +-----------+-----------+
            |                                       |
            +-------------------+-------------------+
                                |
                        (Fan-In / Verification)
                                |
                                v
                  +---------------------------+
                  |  5. State Aggregator &    |
                  |     Self-Healing Check    |
                  +-------------+-------------+
                                |
                     [Pass]     |     [Fail (e.g., SQL Injection Found)]
                       |        +-----------------------------------+
                       v                                            v
         +---------------------------+                +---------------------------+
         |     Final Assembly        |                |  Loop Back to Coder Agent |
         +---------------------------+                +---------------------------+
Breakdown of the Execution Stages:
Stage 1 (Sequential Start): The Coder Agent runs first because neither security scanning nor documentation can happen without a base code artifact. It outputs raw code and abstract syntax tree (AST) metadata into a shared state object.

Stage 2 (Fan-Out Concurrency): Once the code artifact is committed to the shared state, the orchestrator triggers Node B (Security Auditor) and Node C (Documentation Writer) simultaneously.

Security Auditor parses the code for vulnerabilities, hardcoded secrets, and compliance failures.

Documentation Writer simultaneously extracts function signatures, parameter types, and docstrings to generate clean Markdown or OpenAPI specs.

Stage 3 (Fan-In & Validation): Both agents push their results back to the State Aggregator.

3. State Management & The Self-Healing Loop
A major differentiator for a Tier-1 app is how it handles failures in the graph.

State Object Schema: All agents read from and write to an immutable, version-controlled state dictionary (e.g., LangGraph's StateGraph).

JSON
{
  "task_id": "task_9921",
  "raw_code": "app.post('/login', ...)",
  "security_scan": { "status": "FAILED", "vulnerabilities": ["SQL Injection in line 14"] },
  "documentation": { "status": "COMPLETED", "output": "# Login API..." },
  "retry_count": 1
}
Conditional Edge Evaluation: After the Fan-In phase, a conditional router evaluates the state:

If security_scan.status == "FAILED", the graph automatically triggers a feedback loop. It injects the specific security vulnerability logs back into the Coder Agent, updating the state with a instruction: "Fix the SQL injection on line 14 while preserving the rest of the logic."

This loop repeats until the security check passes (with a hard cap on retries, e.g., max 3 attempts).

If all nodes return SUCCESS, the state aggregator compiles the code, security report, and documentation into a single, unified markdown payload and injects it back into the user's target platform (ChatGPT, Hermes, etc.).

4. Why This Surpasses Enterprise Competitors
Most enterprise bots use naive, single-turn prompting where one large model tries to code, check security, and write docs all in one pass—leading to hallucinations, missed vulnerabilities, and bloated context windows.

By splitting this into a stateful, asynchronous DAG:

Lower Latency via Concurrency: Code documentation and security parsing happen at the exact same time.

Specialized Model Allocation: You can assign a fast, cheap model (like a 70B open-source model) to write documentation, while assigning a heavy reasoning model (like Claude 3.5 Sonnet) to the Coder and Security Auditor nodes.

Zero-Trust Guardrails: Code never reaches the user until it has successfully traversed the automated security evaluation node.

