3. How the Security Auditor Node Executes This Schema
When the orchestration graph reaches the Security Auditor node, it processes the injected skill in three sequential sub-steps:

Deterministic Static Scanning (Fast-Pass):
Before even calling an LLM, the engine runs the static_linting_rules (regex and AST matching) against the Coder Agent's output. If a pattern match triggers (e.g., SQLI-001), the build fails instantly, saving API tokens and time, and returning the exact error message to the Coder Agent for immediate self-correction.

Contextual LLM Security Evaluation:
If the code passes static regex lints, it passes the code along with the system_directive to a heavy reasoning model acting as the Security Auditor. The model evaluates logical vulnerabilities (e.g., broken object-level authorization) that regex can't catch.

Sandbox Test Generation:
The node uses the automated_test_injection template to spin up a secure, sandboxed execution environment (like a lightweight Docker container or WebAssembly runtime), running the security fuzz test against the code to prove it withstands edge-case attacks.

By decoupling these rules into a modular schema, your enterprise customers can write their own custom compliance, linting, and security rules (e.g., internal SOC2 policies) without touching your core application codebase.

