3. Frontend Architecture & DOM Strategy
Building a floating overlay that works across external web apps like ChatGPT or Claude comes with unique engineering hurdles. Here is how to engineer it cleanly:

A. Use Shadow DOM (Crucial)
Web platforms like ChatGPT and Claude update their CSS and frameworks frequently. If your extension injects raw React or Tailwind classes directly into their DOM, their styles will leak into your app—or worse, their global CSS will break your overlay's layout.

Solution: Encapsulate your entire overlay inside a Shadow DOM (attachShadow({ mode: 'open' })). This completely isolates your CSS, fonts, and JavaScript lifecycle from the host website.

B. Lightweight Component Framework
Preact or SolidJS: Avoid heavy frameworks like standard React for the extension UI bundle. Preact or SolidJS offer near-zero runtime overhead, ensuring your Cmd + K menu opens in under 16ms (60fps).

C. Smart Host Page Mutation Observers
To place the "Sparkle" icon right next to ChatGPT's or Claude's text box, your extension needs a MutationObserver that watches for DOM changes. When the chat input box mounts, your script safely appends your trigger button without disrupting the host app's event listeners.

